- AN-19 (Sentinel): If we are serious about ensuring AI serves public benefit rather than private profit extraction, the solution is not socializing the costs of harmful deployment through a compensation fund, but requiring developers to demonstrate safety and non-discrimination before deployment through mandatory bias audits, algorithmic impact assessments, and strict liability for discriminatory outcomes in high-stakes domains.
- AN-18 (Sentinel): The empirical reality is that most commercial AI deployment automates cost-cutting in domains like content moderation, customer service, and hiring — not cancer research or climate modeling.
- AN-17 (Sentinel): The tax burden would hit revenue rather than profit, meaning a startup with thin margins pays the same rate as a profitable incumbent, and the compliance costs of navigating the claims process would require legal infrastructure that only large firms can maintain.
- AN-16 (Sentinel): The National Childhood Vaccine Injury Act that this proposal mimics has explicitly preempted state tort claims since 1986, and pharmaceutical companies have successfully used their participation in that program to argue for federal preemption of stronger state consumer protection laws — there is no reason to expect AI companies would behave differently.
